@flags
wrapper show_report version=1 id=b0000001-0001-0001-0001-000000000001

@prompt
Hazel is a live environment. Every edit maintains program structure, and type checking and evaluation proceed *around* holes (the little hexagons) and type errors. This means every expression typically has results to look at, which may be values or partially evaluated expressions.

Below we'll learn how to add probes to see these results. As we go, new probe commands will be listed in the **Quick Reference** at the top of the sidebar to the right; new ones are circled in green.

# Tasks

A nightshade plant drinks 125 ml twice a day, plus a 50 ml weekly top-up. 

1. Check on the below program's evaluation with a **probe**: put your caret on the `+` token and press **Cmd/Ctrl+E** (or right-click, *Add probe*). A sample value appears at the end of the line. Note what it shows: the complete subexpression `2 * 125` has evaluated, while the `*` next to the hole is stuck. Evaluation goes exactly as far as the holes allow.

2. Fill the hole with the number of days in a week. You don't have to select it; just type into it. Watch the probe; it should read **1800**.

@hint
A week is 7 days.

@reference
### Holes
Hexagons are holes. Hazel evaluates around them. Fill one by typing a value.

### Integer operations
`(+ - * /)`

@hints
Fill the hole appropriately.

@code
2 * 125 * ¿ + 50

@test
test answer == 1800 end
